\\right) \\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right) \\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right) = \\left|a\\right|\\frac{1 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 5}{2^3} = \\left|a\\right|\\frac{15}{8}\n   \\]\n   The value calculated here immediately shows that the product of the distances is larger than \\( \\frac{15}{8} \\), meaning \\( |a| \\) will play a significant role in further increasing this value.\n\n6. **Justify for larger n**:\n\n   For larger values of \\( n \\) (more than six roots), the contributions of additional roots will further increase \\( \\left|P\\left(x_i\\right)\\right| \\).\n\n7. **Conclusion about the value of \\( |P(x)| > 3 \\)**:\n\n   Since our minimal scenario already gives us a value safely greater than 3, adjustments by additional terms will only exceed this threshold significantly without loss of generality.\n\nThus, given \\( n \\geq 6 \\) distinct integer roots, when the graph of \\( P(x) \\) is translated by 3 units upward or downward, the number of intersections with the x-axis remains \\( n \\).\n\n### Conclusion:\nThe polynomial \\( P(x) + 3 \\) will therefore have \\( n \\) distinct real roots.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{Polynomial } P(x) + 3 \\text{ has } n \\text{ distinct real roots.}}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "On a square island, there are several countries. Is it possible to divide these countries into smaller ones in such a way that no new points of intersection of boundaries appear, and so that the map of this island can be colored in two colors (countries sharing a common border section must be colored in different colors)?", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I need to figure out if it's possible to divide the countries on a square island into smaller countries without creating new boundary intersections and then color the map with two colors. Hmm. Let me break this down step by step.\n\nFirst, the problem states that there are several countries on a square island. The goal is to divide these countries into smaller ones such that two conditions are met: \n\n1. No new points where boundaries intersect are introduced. So, after division, all the boundaries should only meet at existing intersection points. That probably means that when we split a country, the new borders we draw shouldn't create new corners or junctions where three or more countries meet. All existing intersections must remain the same, and new divisions can only add borders that connect to existing points or extend between existing boundaries without forming new crossings.\n\n2. The resulting map should be 2-colorable. That means that if two countries share a common border segment (not just a point), they must be colored differently. And with two colors, this should be possible without any conflicts.\n\nAlright, so the question is whether such a division is possible for any initial configuration of countries on the square island.\n\nLet me recall some graph theory. A map that's 2-colorable must be a bipartite graph. In a bipartite graph, the vertices can be divided into two sets such that every edge connects a vertex from one set to the other. For a map, this would mean that the countries can be divided into two groups where adjacent countries are in different groups.\n\nBut not all maps are bipartite. For example, a map with an odd-numbered cycle (like three countries each bordering the other two) cannot be 2-colored. However, the problem allows us to divide existing countries into smaller ones. So maybe by splitting countries, we can eliminate any odd cycles, thereby making the graph bipartite.\n\nBut the constraint is that we can't introduce new boundary intersection points. So how does that affect the division? If we split a country, the new borders must connect existing boundary points. So, if a country is a polygon, we can split it by drawing a polygonal line from one existing vertex to another, but not creating any new vertices where three or more boundaries meet.\n\nWait, but if we split a country by drawing a line from an existing vertex to another, that might create a new edge but not a new vertex. However, if the division requires creating a new vertex (i.e., a point where three or more boundaries meet), that's prohibited. So all new boundaries must only connect existing vertices.\n\nBut in that case, splitting a country would be like partitioning a polygon into smaller polygons by connecting non-adjacent vertices with straight lines (or along existing edges). But since the island is a square, all existing boundaries are part of the square's edges or internal boundaries.\n\nWait, perhaps the key is that the original map's dual graph (where each country is a vertex and edges represent shared borders) may have odd cycles, but by subdividing countries, we can convert it into a bipartite graph. But we have to do this without introducing new intersection points.\n\nAlternatively, maybe we can always perform such a division. For example, if the original map isn't 2-colorable, perhaps by splitting some countries into smaller ones along existing boundaries (without creating new intersections), we can break the odd cycles.\n\nLet me think of a simple example. Suppose the original map is a chessboard pattern, already 2-colorable. Then no division is needed. But if the original map has an odd cycle, say three countries forming a triangle, each sharing a border with the other two. Then this cannot be 2-colored. If we can divide one of these countries into two, such that the triangle becomes a square or another even cycle. For example, split one country into two, so that the cycle becomes four countries. Then it's 2-colorable.\n\nBut how to split without creating new boundary intersections. Suppose the three countries meet at a single point. Wait, if three countries meet at a point, that's a vertex where three boundaries meet. If we split one of them, say Country A, by drawing a border from that common vertex to another existing vertex. Wait, but if Country A is adjacent to Countries B and C, splitting A into A1 and A2 would require that the new border starts at the common vertex and ends at another existing vertex. If there's no other existing vertex on Country A's boundary, perhaps we can't split it without creating a new intersection point.\n\nHmm, maybe this is more complicated. Let's consider the original map. All boundary intersections are the vertices of the original countries. So when we divide a country, we can only connect existing vertices with new borders. So if a country is a polygon with vertices V1, V2, ..., Vn, then splitting it would involve drawing a diagonal from Vi to Vj, as long as that diagonal doesn't cross any existing borders or create new intersections.\n\nWait, but if I draw a diagonal inside a country, connecting two of its existing vertices, that would split the country into two, and the new border is just between the two new countries. This doesn't introduce any new intersection points because the endpoints are already existing vertices, and the new edge is entirely within the original country. So maybe this is allowed.\n\nIf that's the case, then perhaps any country can be split into smaller countries by drawing such non-intersecting diagonals between existing vertices, without creating new intersection points. Then, if the original map has an odd cycle, we can split a country in the cycle to turn it into an even cycle.\n\nBut does splitting a country in an odd cycle necessarily remove the odd cycle? Let's see. Suppose we have a triangle of three countries A, B, C. If we split country A into A1 and A2, then the adjacency might become A1 adjacent to B and A2, and A2 adjacent to C and A1. Then the cycle would now be A1, B, C, A2, which is a cycle of length 4, which is even. So yes, splitting a country in an odd cycle can turn it into an even cycle.\n\nTherefore, in general, if we can split countries involved in odd cycles into smaller countries, thereby converting all odd cycles into even ones, the graph becomes bipartite, hence 2-colorable.\n\nBut we need to ensure that this can be done for any initial configuration, and that such splits don't introduce new intersection points. Since each split is done by connecting existing vertices within a country, it shouldn't create new intersections. The new borders are internal to the original country, so they only meet existing borders at the existing vertices.\n\nTherefore, it seems possible. For each odd cycle in the original map, identify a country in that cycle and split it into two, thereby breaking the odd cycle into an even cycle. Repeat this process until all cycles are even, resulting in a bipartite graph which is 2-colorable.\n\nBut wait, need to make sure that splitting a country in an odd cycle doesn't inadvertently create other odd cycles. However, splitting a country into two would replace the original country's node in the dual graph with two nodes connected by an edge. In the cycle, replacing one node with two connected nodes would increase the cycle length by one, turning an odd cycle into an even one. For example, a triangle (3-cycle) becomes a square (4-cycle) after splitting one country into two.\n\nSimilarly, a pentagon (5-cycle) would become a hexagon (6-cycle), which is even. So this seems to work. Therefore, by systematically splitting countries involved in odd cycles, we can convert all cycles to even length, resulting in a bipartite graph.\n\nSince we can perform this splitting without introducing new intersection points (by connecting existing vertices), the answer should be yes, it's possible.\n\nWait, but the problem says \"several countries\" on a square island. So the initial number is arbitrary. But the process should still apply regardless of the initial number. As long as we can split countries to eliminate odd cycles, the map becomes 2-colorable.\n\nTherefore, the final answer is yes, it is possible.", "deepseek_solution": "Yes, it is possible to divide the countries on the square island into smaller ones without introducing new boundary intersection points such that the map becomes 2-colorable. Here's the systematic reasoning:\n\n1. **Graph Representation**: Treat the map as a planar graph where countries are vertices, and shared borders are edges. For the map to be 2-colorable, this graph must be bipartite (no odd-length cycles).\n\n2. **Odd Cycle Elimination**: If the original graph contains odd cycles, split countries within these cycles. For example, a triangular cycle (3 countries) can be converted into a quadrilateral (4 countries) by dividing one country into two smaller countries using a diagonal between existing boundary vertices. This breaks the odd cycle into an even one without creating new intersection points.\n\n3. **Splitting Process**: By iteratively splitting countries involved in odd cycles, all cycles in the graph are transformed into even-length cycles. Each split connects existing vertices within a country, ensuring no new boundary intersections are introduced.\n\n4. **Bipartite Result**: Once all cycles are even, the graph becomes bipartite. The map can then be colored with two colors such that adjacent countries have distinct colors.\n\nThus, through strategic subdivision of countries along existing vertices, the map can be reconfigured to meet both requirements: no new boundary intersections and 2-colorability.\n\n**Final Answer**\n\\boxed{Yes}", "ground_truth_solution": "### Problem:\n\n**Question 1:** On a square island, there are several countries. Can these countries be subdivided so that no new points appear where borders intersect, and so that the map of this island can be colored in two colors (countries sharing a border segment should be colored differently)?\n\nTo determine if the countries on the island can be subdivided as described, we need to delve into graph theory and the concept of planar graphs. Specifically, the question is about whether the island\u2019s representation as a graph can be tr