tic in \\( t \\): \\( 3t^2 -13a t + 4a^2 \\). Let's try to factor this quadratic:\n\nLooking for factors of \\( 3 \\times 4a^2 = 12a^2 \\) that add up to -13a. Hmm, factors would need to be two terms that multiply to 12a\u00b2 and add to -13a. Let's see:\n\n-12a and -a: multiply to 12a\u00b2 and add to -13a. Yes, that works.\n\nSo, \\( 3t^2 -13a t +4a^2 = 3t^2 -12a t -a t +4a^2 = 3t(t -4a) -a(t -4a) = (3t -a)(t -4a) \\)\n\nAh, nice! So, the quadratic factors as \\( (3t - a)(t - 4a) \\).\n\nTherefore, the equation becomes:\n\n\\( (3t - a)e^t + (3t - a)(t -4a) = 0 \\)\n\nFactor out \\( (3t - a) \\):\n\n\\( (3t - a)(e^t + t -4a) = 0 \\)\n\nSo, the equation factors into two parts: \\( 3t - a = 0 \\) or \\( e^t + t -4a = 0 \\).\n\nTherefore, the solutions for \\( t \\) are:\n\n1. \\( 3t - a = 0 \\implies t = \\frac{a}{3} \\)\n2. \\( e^t + t -4a = 0 \\implies e^t + t = 4a \\)\n\nSince \\( t = \\log x \\), each solution for \\( t \\) corresponds to a solution for \\( x = e^t \\). Therefore, the original equation will have solutions when either \\( t = \\frac{a}{3} \\) or when \\( e^t + t = 4a \\). For the original equation to have a unique solution, these two equations must have exactly one solution in total. This can happen in two ways:\n\n1. Either one of the equations has exactly one solution and the other has no solutions.\n2. Both equations have solutions, but they coincide (i.e., the same \\( t \\) satisfies both), so that the total number of solutions is one instead of two.\n\nSo, we need to analyze both possibilities.\n\nFirst, let's consider the first equation: \\( t = \\frac{a}{3} \\). This gives a unique solution \\( t = \\frac{a}{3} \\), which corresponds to \\( x = e^{a/3} \\). So, this is always one solution, provided that \\( x = e^{a/3} \\) is valid. Since \\( x > 0 \\) (as \\( \\log x \\) is defined), this is always valid for any real \\( a \\).\n\nNow, the second equation: \\( e^t + t = 4a \\). Let's analyze the number of solutions to this equation. Let\u