our, that would be 10 buses? Wait, from 6:30 to 7:30 is one hour, so each hour there's a bus at :30. So from 6:30 AM to 4:30 PM is 10 hours, which would be 10 buses. But wait, let's count them:\n\nStarting at 6:30, then 7:30, 8:30, 9:30, 10:30, 11:30, 12:30, 1:30 PM, 2:30 PM, 3:30 PM, 4:30 PM. Wait, that's 11 buses. Because from 6:30 AM to 4:30 PM is 10 hours, but since it's inclusive, the number of departures is 10 + 1 = 11. Wait, let me check. If you start counting at 6:30, each subsequent hour adds one bus. So 6:30, 7:30 (1), 8:30 (2), ..., 4:30 PM. Let's convert to 24-hour time to avoid confusion. 6:30 is 6:30, 4:30 PM is 16:30. The difference between 6:30 and 16:30 is 10 hours. Since buses leave every hour, the number of departures is 10 + 1 = 11. Because the first bus is at 6:30, then each hour up to 16:30. So 16:30 - 6:30 = 10 hours, which gives 11 buses (including both start and end).\n\nWait, but actually, the formula for the number of terms in an arithmetic sequence with common difference of 1 hour, starting at 6:30 and ending at 16:30 is (16.5 - 6.5)/1 + 1 = 10 + 1 = 11. Yes, that's correct. So 11 buses.\n\nBut wait, let's test with a smaller example. Suppose from 6:30 to 7:30, that's 1 hour, but you have two buses: 6:30 and 7:30. So (7.5 - 6.5)/1 +1 = 1 +1=2. Correct. Similarly, from 6:30 to 8:30 would be 3 buses. So yes, 11 buses.\n\nTherefore, the Moscow bus departing at 12:00 will encounter 11 buses from Oryol. But wait, let me confirm again.\n\nAlternatively, maybe there's another way. The Moscow bus is on the road from 12:00 to 17:00. Any Oryol bus that departs from 6:30 to 16:30 will have overlapping travel times. Because:\n\n- An Oryol bus departing at 6:30 arrives at 11:30. But wait, 6:30 +5 hours=11:30. But our Moscow bus departs at 12:00. So this bus arrives in Moscow at 11:30, which is before our Moscow bus even starts. So they wouldn't meet. Wait a minute, there's a mistake here!\n\nHold on, my earlier calculation said that Y:30 <=17:00 and Y+5:30 >=12:00. So Y >=12:00 -5:30=6:30. But if an Oryol bus departs at 6:30, it arrives at 11:30, which is before our Moscow bus departs at 12:00. So that bus wouldn't be on the road during our Moscow bus's trip. Therefore, my earlier reasoning was incorrect.\n\nSo perhaps the correct overlap is when the Oryol bus departs after (12:00 - 5 hours) =7:00, but since they depart at :30, the first Oryol bus that is still on the road when our Moscow bus departs is the one that departs at 7:30. Because 7:30 +5 hours=12:30, so it arrives at 12:30, which is after our Moscow bus departs at 12:00. Therefore, the Oryol bus departing at 7:30 is on the road from 7:30 to 12:30, which overlaps with our Moscow bus's trip from 12:00 to 17:00. They overlap between 12:00 to 12:30, so they do meet.\n\nSimilarly, the last Oryol bus that our Moscow bus will meet is the one departing at 16:30 (4:30 PM), which arrives at 21:30 (9:30 PM). But our Moscow bus arrives at 17:00, so the Oryol bus departing at 16:30 is on the road from 16:30 to 21:30. Our Moscow bus is on the road until 17:00, so they overlap from 16:30 to 17:00. Thus, they do meet.\n\nTherefore, the correct departure times of Oryol buses are from 7:30 AM up to 4:30 PM. Now let's count these.\n\nFrom 7:30 AM to 4:30 PM. Converting to 24-hour time: 7:30 is 7.5 hours, 16:30 is 16.5 hours. The difference is 16.5 -7.5=9 hours. So the number of buses is 9*2 +1? Wait, no. Since buses depart every hour at :30, from 7:30, 8:30,...,16:30. Let's list them:\n\n7:30, 8:30, 9:30, 10:30, 11:30, 12:30, 13:30, 14:30, 15:30, 16:30. That's from 7:30 to 16:30 inclusive. Let's count: 7:30 is 1, 8:30 is 2, ..., 16:30 is 10. So 10 buses.\n\nWait, from 7:30 to 16:30 is 9 hours (16:30 -7:30=9h). Since a bus departs every hour, the number of departures is 9 +1=10. So 10 buses.\n\nBut earlier, when I thought the range was 6:30 to 16:30, I got 11, but realized that 6:30 arrives at 11:30, which is before our Moscow bus departs. So the correct range is 7:30 to 16:30, which is 10 buses.\n\nBut wait, let's verify with specific times. The Moscow bus departs at 12:00, arrives at 17:00.\n\nOryol buses that depart at 7:30 arrive at 12:30. So this bus is on the road from 7:30 to 12:30. Overlaps with Moscow bus from 12:00 to 12:30. So they meet.\n\nNext Oryol bus departs at 8:30, arrives at 13:30. On the road 8:30-13:30. Overlaps with Moscow bus's entire trip 12:00-17:00 from 12:00-13:30. So they meet.\n\nSimilarly, each subsequent Oryol bus departs every hour at :30. The last one that overlaps is the 16:30 departure, arriving at 21:30. Overlaps with Moscow bus's trip from 16:30-17:00. So they meet.\n\nSo from 7:30 to 16:30, each hour at :30, that's 10 buses (7:30,8:30,...,16:30).\n\nWait, 16:30 -7:30 =9 hours, so 9 intervals, which would be 10 buses. Yes, that's correct.\n\nSo the answer is 10 buses. But wait, let me check again.\n\nAlternatively, think about the time window during which the Oryol buses must depart to overlap with the Moscow bus's trip. The Moscow bus is traveling from T=12:00 to T+5=17:00.\n\nAn Oryol bus departs at Y:30 and arrives at Y:30 +5h = (Y+5):30.\n\nTo overlap with the Moscow bus's trip [12:00,17:00], the Oryol bus's trip [Y:30, (Y+5):30] must intersect with [12:00,17:00].\n\nSo the condition is Y:30 <17:00 and (Y+5):30 >12:00.\n\nWhich simplifies to Y:30 <17:00 => Y <17:00 (but since Y is in hours with :30 departure, Y can be up to 16:30).\n\nAnd (Y+5):30 >12:00 => Y+5 >12:00 -0:30? Wait, let's do the math properly.\n\n(Y+5):30 >12:00 => Y+5 hours +30 minutes >12:00?\n\nWait, no. The arrival time is Y:30 +5 hours = (Y+5):30. So we need (Y+5):30 >12:00.\n\nSimilarly, the departure time Y:30 must be <17:00.\n\nSo:\n\n(Y+5):30 >12:00 => Y+5 >12 -0:30? Wait, let's convert everything to minutes past midnight for clarity.\n\nLet\u2019s convert all times to minutes:\n\nMoscow bus departs at 12:00 = 720 minutes (12*60).\n\nArrives at 17:00 = 1020 minutes.\n\nAn Oryol bus departs at Y hours and 30 minutes = 