: null}, {"problem": "Prove the inequality\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{1 \\cdot \\sqrt{2}+2 \\cdot \\sqrt{1}}+\\frac{1}{2 \\cdot \\sqrt{3}+3 \\cdot \\sqrt{2}}+\\ldots+\\frac{1}{2012 \\cdot \\sqrt{2013}+2013 \\cdot \\sqrt{2012}}>0,97\n$$", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I need to prove that this sum is greater than 0.97. The sum is from n=1 to 2012 of 1 divided by [n times sqrt(n+1) plus (n+1) times sqrt(n)]. Hmm, that looks complicated, but maybe there's a pattern or a way to simplify each term. Let me write out the general term first:\n\nTerm_n = 1 / [n * sqrt(n+1) + (n+1) * sqrt(n)]\n\nHmm. Let me see. Maybe I can rationalize the denominator? If I multiply numerator and denominator by the conjugate, that might help. The conjugate of the denominator would be [n * sqrt(n+1) - (n+1) * sqrt(n)], right? Let's try that.\n\nSo multiply numerator and denominator by [n * sqrt(n+1) - (n+1) * sqrt(n)]:\n\nTerm_n = [n * sqrt(n+1) - (n+1) * sqrt(n)] / [ (n * sqrt(n+1))^2 - ((n+1) * sqrt(n))^2 ]\n\nLet me compute the denominator:\n\n(n^2 * (n+1)) - ((n+1)^2 * n) = n^3 + n^2 - (n^3 + 2n^2 + n) = n^3 + n^2 - n^3 - 2n^2 - n = -n^2 - n\n\nSo the denominator simplifies to -n(n + 1). Therefore, the term becomes:\n\nTerm_n = [n * sqrt(n+1) - (n+1) * sqrt(n)] / [-n(n + 1)] = - [n * sqrt(n+1) - (n+1) * sqrt(n)] / [n(n + 1)]\n\nLet me split the numerator:\n\n= - [n * sqrt(n+1) / (n(n + 1)) - (n+1) * sqrt(n) / (n(n + 1))]\n\nSimplify each term:\n\nFirst part: n * sqrt(n+1) / [n(n + 1)] = sqrt(n+1) / (n + 1) = 1 / sqrt(n + 1)\n\nSecond part: (n+1) * sqrt(n) / [n(n + 1)] = sqrt(n) / n = 1 / sqrt(n)\n\nTherefore, Term_n simplifies to:\n\n- [1 / sqrt(n + 1) - 1 / sqrt(n)] = 1 / sqrt(n) - 1 / sqrt(n + 1)\n\nOh! That's a telescoping term! So each term in the sum is equal to 1/sqrt(n) - 1/sqrt(n + 1). Therefore, when we sum from n=1 to 2012, the sum becomes:\n\nSum = (1/sqrt(1) - 1/sqrt(2)) + (1/sqrt(2) - 1/sqrt(3)) + ... + (1/sqrt(2012) - 1/sqrt(2013))\n\nAll the intermediate terms cancel out, leaving:\n