\nAlternatively, consider the problem as a variant of the Hamiltonian path game, where players alternate extending a path. The player who cannot extend loses. The question is then about the parity of the maximum path length.\n\nBut Hamiltonian path length is n\u00b2 -1 moves (since starting from one square, visiting all others). If n\u00b2 -1 is odd, then the first player will make the last move; if even, the second player. Since n\u00b2 -1 is odd when n is even (since n\u00b2 is even, so even -1 is odd), and n\u00b2 -1 is even when n is odd (odd\u00b2 -1 is even). Therefore, when n is even, the maximum number of moves is odd, so the first player can make the last move if the game goes to the end. When n is odd, the maximum number of moves is even, so the second player makes the last move.\n\nBut this assumes that both players play optimally to extend the path as much as possible. However, in reality, one player might be forced to make a move that shortens the game. But if both players are trying to maximize the number of moves, then the parity of the maximum number of moves determines the winner.\n\nTherefore, if n is even, the maximum number of moves is odd, so first player can win by forcing the game to last all moves. If n is odd, the maximum number is even, so second player can win.\n\nBut the problem is to prove that regardless of strategy, the first or second player can force a win based on parity. So perhaps the key is the parity of the number of squares. But since the game could end before all squares are visited, we need a strategy that ensures the opponent runs out of moves first.\n\nAlternatively, consider the concept of a \"winning strategy\" where a player can always respond to the opponent's move in a way that guarantees a win. For example, in even n, the first player can always pair the moves such that they leave the second player without a move. Or maybe use a pairing strategy where the board is divided into pairs, and each move by the opponent is a