erefore, the maximum possible number of primes is 9. Therefore, Jill's claim of 9 primes is possible, but Jack's claim of 10 primes is impossible because the maximum number of primes you can have is 9. Therefore, Jill is correct.\n\nWait, but hold on. Is that necessarily the case? Because even if we have E=3 and O=3, meaning 3 even and 3 odd numbers, the number of mixed pairs is 9. But for those 9 pairs, each of those sums is an odd number, which could be prime. However, in reality, some of those sums might be composite. So the maximum possible number of primes is 9, but achieving that would require all 9 of those sums to be prime. Is that actually possible?\n\nSo the next step is to check whether there exists a set of 6 distinct positive integers with 3 even and 3 odd numbers such that all 9 of the even-odd pairwise sums are prime. If such a set exists, then Jill's claim is possible, and since Jack claims 10, which is more than the maximum possible, he must be wrong. If no such set exists, then even 9 might not be possible.\n\nTherefore, we need to check if there exists a set of 3 even and 3 odd numbers where all the cross sums are prime.\n\nFirst, note that the even numbers must be distinct, as well as the odd numbers. Let's consider the smallest possible numbers to minimize the sums and make primes more likely.\n\nLet me try to construct such a set.\n\nLet's start with the even numbers. The smallest three distinct even positive integers are 2, 4, 6.\n\nAnd the smallest three distinct odd positive integers are 1, 3, 5.\n\nNow, let's compute all the sums:\n\n2+1=3 (prime)\n\n2+3=5 (prime)\n\n2+5=7 (prime)\n\n4+1=5 (prime)\n\n4+3=7 (prime)\n\n4+5=9 (composite)\n\n6+1=7 (prime)\n\n6+3=9 (composite)\n\n6+5=11 (prime)\n\nSo here, the sums would be: 3,5,7,5,7,9,7,9,11. So primes are 3,5,7,5,7,7,11. That's 7 primes, and two composites (9,9). So in this case, only 7 primes. So this set doesn't work.\n\nHmm. So perhaps choosing 2,4,6 and 1,3,5 isn't good enough because 4+5