2. But a \u2265 b, so a - b = sqrt(c).\n\nSimilarly, from b = (a - c)^2. Since a \u2265 c, a - c = sqrt(b).\n\nFrom a = (b - c)^2. Since b \u2265 c, b - c = sqrt(a).\n\nSo we have:\n\nsqrt(a) = b - c,\n\nsqrt(b) = a - c,\n\nsqrt(c) = a - b.\n\nWait, but from c = (a - b)^2, so sqrt(c) = a - b. But since a \u2265 b, sqrt(c) = a - b.\n\nBut sqrt(c) must be non-negative, which is consistent.\n\nSo let's write these equations:\n\n1) sqrt(a) = b - c\n\n2) sqrt(b) = a - c\n\n3) sqrt(c) = a - b\n\nNow, let's try to express variables in terms of each other.\n\nFrom equation 3: a = b + sqrt(c)\n\nFrom equation 1: sqrt(a) = b - c. But a = b + sqrt(c), so sqrt(b + sqrt(c)) = b - c.\n\nFrom equation 2: sqrt(b) = a - c. But a = b + sqrt(c), so sqrt(b) = (b + sqrt(c)) - c = b + sqrt(c) - c.\n\nSo now we have two equations:\n\nsqrt(b + sqrt(c)) = b - c, (from equation 1 and 3)\n\nsqrt(b) = b + sqrt(c) - c. (from equation 2 and 3)\n\nThis seems complicated, but maybe we can find a substitution.\n\nLet me denote x = sqrt(c). Then c = x\u00b2. Also, from equation 3: sqrt(c) = a - b \u21d2 x = a - b \u21d2 a = b + x.\n\nFrom equation 1: sqrt(a) = b - c. But a = b + x, c = x\u00b2. So sqrt(b + x) = b - x\u00b2.\n\nFrom equation 2: sqrt(b) = a - c = (b + x) - x\u00b2 \u21d2 sqrt(b) = b + x - x\u00b2.\n\nSo now we have two equations in terms of b and x:\n\n1) sqrt(b + x) = b - x\u00b2\n\n2) sqrt(b) = b + x - x\u00b2\n\nLet me try to solve these equations.\n\nLet me denote equation 2: sqrt(b) = b + x - x\u00b2.\n\nLet me rearrange equation 2:\n\nb + x - x\u00b2 - sqrt(b) = 0.\n\nThis seems complex. Maybe express x in terms of b from equation 2 and substitute into equation 1.\n\nFrom equation 2: sqrt(b) = b + x - x\u00b2 \u21d2 x - x\u00b2 = sqrt(b) - b \u21d2 x(1 - x) = sqrt(b) - b.\n\nAlternatively, solve for x:\n\nLet me write equation 2 as:\n\nx\u00b2 - x + (sqrt(b) - b) = 0.\n\nThis is a quadratic in x:\n\nx\u00b2 - x + (sqrt(b) - b) = 0.\n\nSolving for x:\n\nx = [1 \u00b1 sqrt(1 - 4*1