x) = x + 1 \\). For \\( x < -1 \\), this is a decreasing function, so as \\( x \\to -\\infty \\), \\( f(x) \\to -\\infty \\), which would make the inequality hold. Hence, \\( a = 0 \\) is not acceptable.\n\nIf \\( a < 0 \\), the parabola opens downward, so there's no minimum (it goes to negative infinity as \\( x \\to \\pm \\infty \\)), but since \\( x < -1 \\), as \\( x \\to -\\infty \\), the \\( a x^2 \\) term dominates. If \\( a < 0 \\), \\( a x^2 \\) tends to negative infinity, so \\( f(x) \\to -\\infty \\), meaning the inequality would have solutions. Therefore, \\( a < 0 \\) is not acceptable.\n\nTherefore, for Case 2, similar to Case 1, we must have \\( a > 0 \\), and the minimum occurs at the vertex.\n\nCompute the vertex for Case 2. The quadratic is \\( a x^2 + x + (1 + 2a) \\). The vertex occurs at:\n\n\\[\nx = -b/(2a) = -1/(2a)\n\\]\n\nBut we need to check if this vertex lies in the interval \\( x < -1 \\).\n\nSince \\( a > 0 \\), \\( x = -1/(2a) \\) is negative. Let's compare \\( -1/(2a) \\) with -1.\n\nWe need:\n\n\\[\n-1/(2a) < -1 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad 1/(2a) > 1 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad 2a < 1 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad a < 1/2\n\\]\n\nTherefore, if \\( a < 1/2 \\), the vertex \\( x = -1/(2a) < -1 \\), so the vertex is within the interval \\( x < -1 \\). Otherwise, if \\( a \\geq 1/2 \\), the vertex is at \\( x = -1/(2a) \\geq -1 \\), which is not in the interval \\( x < -1 \\). Therefore, in that case, the minimum on \\( x < -1 \\) would occur at the endpoint \\( x = -1 \\).\n\nSo, this splits Case 2 into two subcases:\n\n- **Subcase 2a:** \\( 0 < a < 1/2 \\): vertex at \\( x = -1/(2a) < -1 \\), so the minimum is at the vertex.\n  \n- **Subcase 2b:** \\( a \\geq 1/2 \\): vertex at \\( x = -1/(2a) \\geq -1 \\), so the minimum on \\( x < -1 \\) occurs at \\( x = -1 \\).\n\nTherefore, let's compute the minimum for each subcase.\n\n**Subcase 2a: \\( 0 < a < 1/2 \\)**\n\nMinimum at vertex \\( x = -1/(2a) \\):\n\nCompute \\( f(-1/(2a)) \\):\n\n\\[\n