lue \\( -\\frac{1}{4a} + 1 + 2a \\geq 0 \\) leads to solving \\( 8a^2 + 4a - 1 \\geq 0 \\). The relevant root is \\( a \\geq \\frac{-1 + \\sqrt{3}}{4} \\).\n  - For \\( a \\geq \\frac{1}{2} \\), the minimum value is \\( 3a \\geq 0 \\), which is always true.\n\n**Combining the results:**\n\n- The intersection of the conditions from both cases shows that \\( a \\geq \\frac{1 + \\sqrt{3}}{4} \\) ensures both minima are non-negative.\n\nThus, the range of values for \\( a \\) is \\(\\boxed{\\left[ \\dfrac{1 + \\sqrt{3}}{4}, +\\infty \\right)}\\).", "ground_truth_solution": "\n1. To find the values of \\(a\\) such that the inequality \\(a x^{2}-|x+1|+2a < 0\\) has no solutions, we start by noting that for the inequality to have no real solutions, the expression \\(a x^{2}-|x+1|+2a\\) must be non-negative for all real \\(x\\).\n\n2. Thus, we require:\n   \\[\n   a x^{2}-|x+1|+2a \\geq 0 \\quad \\text{for all } x \\in \\mathbb{R}.\n   \\]\n\n3. Rearrange to isolate the absolute term:\n   \\[\n   a x^{2} + 2a \\geq |x+1|.\n   \\]\n\n4. Define the function \\(f(x) = \\frac{|x+1|}{x^2+2}\\). We need to ensure that:\n   \\[\n   a \\geq f(x) \\quad \\text{for all } x \\in \\mathbb{R}.\n   \\]\n\n5. This implies that \\(a\\) must be greater than or equal to the maximum value of \\(f(x)\\):\n   \\[\n   a \\geq \\max_{x \\in \\mathbb{R}} f(x).\n   \\]\n\n6. To find \\(\\max f(x)\\), we consider two cases for \\(x\\):\n\n   - Case 1: \\(x \\geq -1\\):\n     \\[\n     f(x) = \\frac{x+1}{x^2+2}.\n     \\]\n\n     - Let \\(t = x + 1\\), then \\(t \\geq 0\\) and \\(x = t-1\\):\n       \\[\n       f(t) = \\frac{t}{(t-1)^2 + 2} = \\frac{t}{t^2 - 2t + 1 + 2} = \\frac{t}{t^2 - 2t + 3}.\n       \\]\n\n     - To find the maximum, evaluate the derivative:\n       \\[\n       f'(t) = \\frac{(t^2 - 2t + 3) - t(2t - 2)}{(t^2 - 2t + 3)^2} = \\frac{t^2 - 2t + 3 - 2t^2 + 2t}{(t^2 - 2t + 3)^2} = \\frac{-t^2 + 3}{(t^2 - 2t + 3)^2}.\n       \\]\n\n     - Set \\(f'(t) = 0\\):\n       \\[\n       -t^2 + 3 = 0 \\Rightarrow t^2 = 3 \\Rightarrow t = \\sqrt{3}.\n       \\]\n\n     - Evaluate \\(f(t)\\) at \\(t = \\sqrt{3}\\):\n       \\[\n       f(\\sqrt{3}) = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{(\\sqrt{3})^2 - 2(\\sqrt{3}) + 3} = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3 - 2\\sqrt{3} + 3} = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{6 - 2\\sqrt{3}}.\n       \\]\n\n     - Rationalize the denominator:\n       \\[\n       \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{6 - 2\\sqrt{3}} \\cdot \\frac{6 + 2\\sqrt{3}}{6 + 2\\sqrt{3}} = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}(6 + 2\\sqrt{3})}{(6 - 2\\sqrt{3})(6 + 2\\sqrt{3})} = \\frac{6\\sqrt{3} + 6}{36 - 12} = \\frac{6\\sqrt{3} + 6}{24} = \\frac{\\sqrt{3} + 1}{4}.\n       \\]\n\n   - Case 2: \\(x < -1\\):\n     \\[\n     f(x) = \\frac{-(x+1)}{x^2+2} \\quad \\text{(since \\(x+1 < 0\\))}.\n     \\]\n\n     - Let \\(t = -(x + 1)\\), then \\(t > 0\\) and \\(x = -t-1\\):\n       \\[\n       f(t) = \\frac{t}{(-t-1)^2 + 2} = \\frac{t}{t^2 + 2t + 1 + 2} = \\frac{t}{t^2 + 2t + 3}.\n       \\]\n\n     - The expression is identical to the above, so:\n       \\[\n       \\max f(x) = \\frac{\\sqrt{3} + 1}{4}.\n       \\]\n\n7. Therefore, the maximum value of \\(f(x)\\) for \\(x \\in \\mathbb{R}\\) is:\n   \\[\n   \\max f(x) = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}+1}{4}.\n   \\]\n\n8. Thus, for \\(a x^{2}-|x+1|+2 a \\geq 0\\) for all \\(x\\), we require:\n   \\[\n   a \\geq \\frac{\\sqrt{3}+1}{4}.\n   \\]\n\nConclusion: The range for the values of \\(a\\) is:\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\left[\\frac{\\sqrt{3}+1}{4},+\\infty\\right)}.\n   \\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Assume that Maria Petrovna has `x` liters and Olga Pavlovna has `y` liters of jam left. The numbers \\( x \\) and \\( y \\) are randomly and independently chosen from the interval from 0 to 1. A random point with coordinates \\( (x, y) \\) is chosen from the unit square \\( F \\). When Maria Petrovna ate half of her remaining jam, she had \\( \\frac{x}{2} \\) liters of jam left. Therefore, the event \\( A \\) \u201cMaria Petrovna and Olga Pavlo