er \\( n \\), the inequality \\( \\sum_{k=1}^{n} \\frac{a_{k}}{k^{2}} \\geqslant \\sum_{k=1}^{n} \\frac{1}{k} \\) holds, where \\( a_1, a_2, \\ldots, a_n \\) are pairwise distinct natural numbers, we analyze the minimal configuration of the sequence \\( \\{a_k\\} \\).\n\n**Key Steps:**\n\n1. **Distinct Natural Numbers Constraint:**  \n   The sequence \\( \\{a_k\\} \\) consists of distinct natural numbers. The smallest possible values for \\( a_1, a_2, \\ldots, a_n \\) are \\( 1, 2, \\ldots, n \\). Any deviation from these values (e.g., using larger numbers) will only increase the sum \\( \\sum_{k=1}^{n} \\frac{a_k}{k^2} \\).\n\n2. **Rearrangement Inequality:**  \n   To minimize the weighted sum \\( \\sum_{k=1}^{n} \\frac{a_k}{k^2} \\), pair the smallest \\( a_k \\) with the largest weights \\( \\frac{1}{k^2} \\). Since the weights \\( \\frac{1}{k^2} \\) decrease as \\( k \\) increases, the minimal sum occurs when \\( a_k = k \\) (ascending order). This results in:\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{k=1}^{n} \\frac{k}{k^2} = \\sum_{k=1}^{n} \\frac{1}{k},\n   \\]\n   which equals the right-hand side of the inequality.\n\n3. **General Case:**  \n   If any \\( a_k \\) is greater than \\( k \\), the term \\( \\frac{a_k}{k^2} \\) increases compared to \\( \\frac{1}{k} \\). Since all \\( a_k \\) must be distinct, substituting any smaller missing number (if a larger number is used) would further reduce the sum, confirming that the minimal configuration is indeed \\( a_k = k \\).\n\n**Conclusion:**  \nFor any sequence of distinct natural numbers \\( \\{a_k\\} \\), the sum \\( \\sum_{k=1}^{n} \\frac{a_k}{k^2} \\) is minimized when \\( a_k = k \\). This minimal sum equals the harmonic series \\( \\sum_{k=1}^{n} \\frac{1}{k} \\). Therefore, the inequality holds for all such sequences.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\sum_{k=1}^{n} \\frac{a_{k}}{k^{2}} \\geqslant \\sum_{k=1}^{n} \\frac{1}{k}}\n\\]", "ground_truth_solution": "\n**Proof using Method 1:**\n\n1. **Rewrite the series**:\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{k=1}^{n}