03b1 + sin \u03b2 (from earlier), we need to maximize sin \u03b1 + sin \u03b2 under the constraint:\n\n1/b\u00b2 + cos \u03b1 + (1/b\u00b2) cos \u03b2 + cos(\u03b1 - \u03b2) = 0  \n\nThis seems very difficult to solve directly. Perhaps there's a symmetry or a specific case where \u03b1 = \u03b2. Let\u2019s assume \u03b1 = \u03b2. Then the equation becomes:\n\n1/b\u00b2 + cos \u03b1 + (1/b\u00b2) cos \u03b1 + cos(0) = 0  \n= 1/b\u00b2 + cos \u03b1 + (1/b\u00b2) cos \u03b1 + 1 = 0  \n= (1/b\u00b2)(1 + cos \u03b1) + cos \u03b1 + 1 = 0  \nFactor:\n\n(1 + cos \u03b1)(1/b\u00b2 + 1) + 0 = 0  \nWhich implies (1 + cos \u03b1)(1 + 1/b\u00b2) = 0  \n\nSince 1 + 1/b\u00b2 > 0 for all real b \u2260 0, this implies 1 + cos \u03b1 = 0 \u21d2 cos \u03b1 = -1 \u21d2 \u03b1 = \u03c0 radians (180 degrees). But if \u03b1 = 180 degrees, then sin \u03b1 = 0, leading to area 0. Which is the minimum, not maximum. Therefore, this case is not useful. \n\nAlternatively, suppose \u03b1 = -\u03b2. Then cos(\u03b1 - \u03b2) = cos(2\u03b1). The equation becomes:\n\n1/b\u00b2 + cos \u03b1 + (1/b\u00b2) cos(-\u03b1) + cos(2\u03b1) = 0  \n= 1/b\u00b2 + cos \u03b1 + (1/b\u00b2) cos \u03b1 + cos(2\u03b1) = 0  \n= (1 + 1/b\u00b2) cos \u03b1 + 1/b\u00b2 + cos(2\u03b1) = 0  \n\nUsing cos(2\u03b1) = 2 cos\u00b2 \u03b1 - 1:\n\n(1 + 1/b\u00b2) cos \u03b1 + 1/b\u00b2 + 2 cos\u00b2 \u03b1 - 1 = 0  \nSimplify:\n\n2 cos\u00b2 \u03b1 + (1 + 1/b\u00b2) cos \u03b1 + 1/b\u00b2 - 1 = 0  \n\nThis quadratic in cos \u03b1 could be solved, but it's still complex. \n\nAlternatively, take b = 1. Then the equation becomes:\n\n1 + cos \u03b1 + cos \u03b2 + cos(\u03b1 - \u03b2) = 0  \nIf b = 1, then sides are 1,1,1,1. The constraint becomes:\n\n1 + cos \u03b1 + cos \u03b2 + cos(\u03b1 - \u03b2) = 0  \n\nBut for a square, \u03b1 = \u03b2 = 90 degrees, then:\n\n1 + 0 + 0 + cos(0) = 1 + 1 = 2 \u2260 0. This contradicts. Wait, but earlier we found that when b = 1, the area is 1. But according to this equation, it's not satisfied. There must be an error. \n\nWait, when b = 1, the sides are all 1, so it's a rhombus. The angles \u03b1 and \u03b2 in the coordinate system would correspond to internal angles of the rhombus. In a rhombus with side 1 and angle \u03b8, the area is sin \u03b8. The maximum area is 1 when \u03b8 = 90 degrees (a square). But according to the equation derived earlier, for b = 1, we have:\n\n1/b\u00b2 + cos \u03b1 + (1/b\u00b2) cos \u03b2 + cos(\u03b1 - \u03b2) = 0  \nWhich becomes:\n\n1 + cos \u03b1 + cos \u03b2 + cos(\u03b1 - \u03b2) = 0  \n\nFor a square, \u03b1 = \u03b2 = 90 degrees:\n\n1 + 0 + 0 + cos(0) = 1 + 1 = 2 \u2260 0  \n\nThis suggests a contradiction. Therefore, my earlier analysis must have an error. \n\nWait, perhaps the issue is in the coordinate system setup. When we derived the equation, we assumed specific positions for the points, but when b = 1 and the quadrilateral is a square, the coordinates would be different. Let me re-examine the coordinate approach. \n\nIf b = 1, the sides are 1,1,1,1. Placing point A at (0,0), point B at (1,0). Then point C is at (1 + cos \u03b1, sin \u03b1). Point D must satisfy distance from C to D is 1 and distance from D to A is 1. For a square, point C would be (1,1), point D would be (0,1), which satisfies both distances. \n\nIn this case, \u03b1 = 90 degrees, \u03b2 = 90 degrees. Plugging into the equation:\n\n1 + cos \u03b1 + cos \u03b2 + cos(\u03b1 - \u03b2) = 1 + 0 + 0 + cos(0) = 1 + 1 = 2 \u2260 0.  \n\nBut according to the equation derived earlier, this should equal zero. Therefore, there must be a mistake in the derivation of the equation. \n\nGoing back to the coordinate system approach, when we derived the equation:\n\n1/b\u00b2 + cos \u03b1 + (1/b\u00b2) cos \u03b2 + cos(\u03b1 - \u03b2) = 0  \n\nThis was supposed to be the condition that point D lies on the origin. However, in the case of the square (b=1), this condition isn't met, which suggests an error in the earlier steps. \n\nLet me revisit the earlier